FlintNotes on Debian
====================

Connect the Windows app (or a phone):   flintnotes pair
Version and updates:                    flintnotes status | flintnotes update
Logs:                                   journalctl -u flintnotes
Settings:                               /etc/default/flintnotes

Notes that already exist on this computer (an Obsidian vault in someone's home, which Samba shares to Windows):

    sudo flintnotes vault /home/name/obsidian

The service then runs as the owner of that folder (the one Samba writes as) and sees nothing else of the home; the folder
is opened as the vault. `flintnotes vault <folder> --print` shows the change without making it, `sudo flintnotes vault
--remove` undoes it. The files stay exactly as they are: nothing is copied or moved.

Vaults the service makes itself live in /var/lib/flintnotes/vaults. To use the same folder from Windows (Obsidian on the same notes), share it
with Samba, writing as the service's user, for example in /etc/samba/smb.conf:

    [FlintNotes]
        path = /var/lib/flintnotes/vaults
        read only = no
        force user = flintnotes
        force group = flintnotes
        create mask = 0664
        directory mask = 0775

Obsidian writes and removes files itself, so FlintNotes only sees what the share has finished writing.
The key of `flintnotes pair` opens every note over a network without encryption: keep the service on the home network
(FLINT_BIND in /etc/default/flintnotes names the card), and rotate the key with `flintnotes pair --rotate`.
